# Commit completed tasks

When a **discrete task is finished** (feature slice, bugfix, polish pass, design/docs delivery the user accepted, etc.), **create a git commit** as part of finishing — do not leave the completed work uncommitted and do not wait for a separate “please commit” message.

## What counts as done

- The agreed scope for that task is implemented (or docs-only delivery is written)
- Relevant verification you already owe for the task has been run (tests/goldens/smoke as appropriate)
- Living docs (`docs/architecture.md`, specs) are updated in the **same** change when the architecture rule requires it

## How to commit

Follow the repo’s normal git safety protocol (no force push, no config changes, no secrets, HEREDOC/here-string commit messages, verify with `git status` after). Prefer **one commit per completed task**; exclude unrelated WIP, scratch fidelity dumps, and secrets.

## Overrides

- **User says not to commit** / “leave uncommitted” / “don’t commit yet” → do not commit
- **Ambiguous whether the task is complete** → ask once, then commit when they confirm done
- Still never push unless the user asks

This project rule **overrides** the generic habit of only committing when explicitly asked.
